it was a muted day in the capital the  threat of russian airstrikes hung over  independence day as did the memory of  those killed fighting in the defense of  the country's freedom  britain has been one of ukraine's  closest allies during the six month war  it is no exaggeration to say that boris  johnson is adored by many ukrainians and  he came here in secret today to mark the  anniversary  this is now a war  for that independence  and history teaches us that when a  country has  a language an identity  a pride  a love of its traditions  a patriotic feeling that simply grows  with every month and year  that passes  when a country of that kind is engaged  in a war  for its very existence  i tell you my friends  that war  is only going to end  one way  ukraine will win  some leaders have already saying that  we've done so much what do you want what  else do you want from me now the prime  minister johnson  always said what else the uk can do for  for ukraine to win  as ukraine will definitely be victorious  i think that's the major essence of the  relations of uk to to ukraine and this  respect and the friendly relations in  between of us  normally an annual military parade would  take place on the vast boulevard that  runs through the centre of kiev  independence from the soviet union is  something they celebrate  instead the street was full of russian  weapons destroyed during their doomed  advance of the city in those frightening  days that started in late february  they are now monuments to russian  failure and a symbol of ukrainian  resistance  vladimir putin thought that russian  forces would capture kiev within days  they failed this  is not the military parade that he  imagined  instead his forces are bogged down in a  war  that they're unwilling to leave and so  far incapable of winning  on the front line the fighting continued  mikolaiv in the south is bombed every  day  barely a street is untouched  an airstrike on mikolayev blew out the  rear bova's windows onto their sleeping  granddaughter  the windows are now boarded up which  gives them some protection but of course  it also blocks the light so their  electricity is on more than normal and  that costs them money they don't have  numbers  she shows us what little they have left  the month they couldn't even afford  bread this morning  every morning people in the city venture  outside to see who is alive and who is  not  that's been the grim reality of life  here  for six long months  alastair bunker sky news  in ukraine
 

